## Day 3 (Friday 16 October) – AI Readiness: Towards Rigorous and Trustworthy AI in Science

Auditorium, 10:15. Chair: Florian Jug (Human Technopole)

### Talk Mercè Crosas

(Mercè Crosas, Director, Computational Social Science and Humanities Laboratory, Barcelona Supercomputing Center, and President of CODATA)

![Slides][slides-icon] [Slides](link)

![Outline](slides/00_outline.jpg)

![AI Adoption Surges Across All Domains](slides/01_ai_adoption_slide.png)

* Slide title: "AI Adoption Surges Across All Domains" (section "01. AI in Science")
* Study scope: analysis of 225M academic works from the 1960s to 2024
* Computer science has been the leading field since the 1990s
* Adoption of AI methods has expanded rapidly across fields since 2016
* 2024 AI adoption rates per domain and leading field:
  * Physical Sciences & Engineering: 10.7% (Computer Science: 23.0%)
  * Life Sciences: 4.4% (Neuroscience: 10.0%)
  * Health Sciences: 3.7% (Cardiology: 5.4%)
  * Social Sciences: 2.8% (Business Management: 5.7%)

  **=>** AI adoption is growing in every domain, with the sharpest rise after 2016, though computer science remains far ahead of the other fields.

> * Castro, Giner, Crosas (2026). [When AI Meets Science](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.06033). arXiv.


#### 01. AI in Science

![Spectrum of AI engagement in research](slides/01_spectrum_of_ai_engagement.jpg)

* Defines AI broadly, beyond the latest large language models
* Low engagement treats AI as a black box: a broad user base uses it as a support tool for literature and data searches, peer reviews and research reasoning
* High engagement means understanding the model itself: specialised researchers use AI as a method for analysis and discovery, foundation model development, model modification (fine-tuning, RAG) and inference execution
* The study focuses explicitly on high-engagement adoption

  **=>** The talk's main analytical focus is on researchers who go beyond using AI as a black box.

#### 02. Science Values

![Scientific theories and social practice](slides/02_scientific_theories_and_social_practice.jpg)

* Science is the pursuit of good explanations, the "beginning of infinity" in knowledge creation (Deutsch)
* Good explanations arise from creativity, not only induction and empiricism; fallibilism is fundamental; authority is rejected (*nullius in verba*); prediction alone is not enough
* Science is a social enterprise governed by norms: public procedures so methods and data can be verified and replicated, accompanied uncertainty, unity in method, and recognition through proper credit ("Citation, citation, citation")

  **=>** Prediction without resilient explanations is not real progress.

![Rapid AI adoption poses threats to science and open science](slides/02_rapid_ai_adoption_threats.jpg)

* Threats to science: disqualification (loss of domain knowledge), disorder (surge of unverified results), motivation loss and conformism, and hyper-empiricism (data without theory)
* Threats to open science: methodological opacity, replication difficulty, open data exploitation by unaligned commercial scraping, and big tech monopolisation of public research infrastructure

  **=>** Rapid AI adoption threatens both the quality of science and the openness of its infrastructure.

![Addressing threats starts by answering key questions](slides/02_addressing_threats_key_questions.jpg)

* Four key questions: applicability and purpose; validation and transparency; data and governance; human agency and equality

#### 03. European Context

![AI and data regulations to achieve AI and data strategies](slides/03_ai_and_data_regulations.jpg)

* DSA: disclosure and vetted data access for researchers and regulators (early August 2023, full February 2024)
* DMA: behavioural obligations on gatekeepers (in force since May 2022)
* AI Act: technical documentation along the value chain, staggered from August 2026
* GDPR: personal data processing rules (enforcement since 2018)
* DGA: neutral public data intermediaries framework (enforcement since 2022)
* Data Act: data portability and fair access, partial in 2025, full in 2026 to 2027

  **=>** The regulations exist, but there is a risk they lack enforcement as AI expands.

![AI factories and data labs](slides/03_ai_factories_and_data_labs.jpg)

* 19 core AI factories and 13 connected antennas across the EuroHPC network, offering compute (GPUs), models, data services, user support, training and domain expert advice
* Seven sectoral data labs: cybersecurity and internal security, climate and environment, language and culture, health and life sciences, manufacturing and robotics, public administration, and research and science
* Goal: AI-ready data and connectors from data spaces to AI factories

#### 04. Advancing AI Readiness: The AI4SOCIAL+ Project

![Five interrelated streams to advance AI readiness](slides/04_five_interrelated_streams.jpg)

* Co-create: Stream A, the AI Readiness Common Hub (ARCH) framework; Stream B, machine-actionable data tools; Stream C, an AI-ready infrastructure platform
* Validate: Stream D, five AI-based social and humanities research use cases
* Upscale: Stream E, an AI4SOCIAL+ Competence Centre leveraging CESSDA and SSHOC
* Builds on existing standards and platforms including Croissant, CDIF, RO-Crate, W3C ODRL, DDI-CDI, COMPSs, Galaxy Europe and Onedata

![Five research use cases to validate AI readiness](slides/04_five_research_use_cases.jpg)

* Use case 01: AI for job trajectories (career paths, labour market dynamics, skill transitions)
* Use case 02: AI in historical archives (transcribing and unlocking cultural and historical records)
* Use case 03: AI for climate and health policies (informing choices at the intersection of public health and environment)
* Use case 04: AI for social simulations (modelling complex social behaviour with multi-agent models)
* Use case 05: beyond academic silos (connecting works across domains through AI graphs)

#### 05. Recommendations

![Recommendation 1: Coordinate AI efforts across EU initiatives](slides/05_recommendation_1_coordinate_ai.jpg)

* The EU already has the ingredients: data and AI regulations and initiatives that can support a trustworthy, sustainable research ecosystem
* Recommendations to EOSC for alignment: coordinated calls with RAISE and AI Factories; connected data repositories and access across EOSC, Data Labs and Data Spaces; standardised AI readiness using the AI4SOCIAL+ framework; integration with Destination Earth for sustainable AI; co-located events; and targeted funding to study AI's impact on science, equality, labour and social interaction
* Key initiatives to align: EOSC, RAISE, AI Factories and Data Labs, and Destination Earth

![Recommendation 2: Co-define declarations on AI and science](slides/05_recommendation_2_co_define_declarations.jpg)

* Precedent: the Leiden Declaration on AI and Mathematics (2026), which bases its recommendations on the values of mathematical research it aims to preserve: disclose automated tools, retain human accountability, defend open science and preserve authorship credit (https://leidendeclaration.ai)
* Call to scientific communities: avoid becoming passive AI users, decide how to integrate AI, express what type of AI the domain needs, address community-specific needs, find commonalities with other fields, and consider impacts and global contexts
* AI4SOCIAL+ plan: co-create declarations on AI for the social sciences and humanities

  **=>** Each research community should set out its own declaration on AI to protect its disciplinary values, credit and human accountability.

![Recommendation 3: Enforceable data contracts and APIs for AI](slides/05_recommendation_3_enforceable_data_contracts.jpg)

* Precedent: the Wikimedia enterprise model, which stopped open scraping by commercial AI companies, offered paid API contracts for high-volume use, and kept public content free for everyone else
* Recommendations to research data repositories: balance open science with commercial AI usage limits and contracts, create dedicated AI APIs while keeping open researcher access, embed technical enforcement inside repositories, advocate supportive regulation, enforce model-level access criteria, and use Wikimedia's open-source API services
* Potential access conditions for AI models: restrict to scientific models, require trustworthy AI compliance, and allow only sustainable (green) AI

  **=>** Repositories should use enforceable data contracts to govern AI training access while keeping research data open to researchers.

#### Summary & Takeaways

![Summary and takeaways](slides/05_summary_takeaways.jpg)

* Balanced AI integration: AI is transforming research quickly, so opportunities must be weighed against risks
* Standardised AI readiness: the AI4SOCIAL+ project helps define a framework that delivers lasting value for science and society
* Coordinated EU initiatives: regulation alone is not enough; strategic alignment across computing, data, skills and funding is vital
* Community AI declarations: research communities must establish declarations that preserve disciplinary values, credit and human accountability
* Enforceable data contracts: repositories should implement data contracts and structured APIs that regulate AI training access, balanced with open science

